  "assumption_basis": {
    "audience": "Expanded cohorts contain 140 million unique people with 200 million genre memberships, including the existing 80 million sports fans. One annual bundle charged once; additional membership services sold to a subset of paid viewers. The shared-audience case admits no new fans or increase in viewing yield.",
    "costs": "Existing physical sport purchases retained, but portfolio commercial and acquisition costs recomputed once. Fixed capture services exclude employee payroll and variable postproduction/distribution services. Pay is the larger of baseline staffing costs or 20% of remote receipts, allocated among resident/visiting staff in the baseline pay proportions.",
    "capacity": "Additional 32 residents and 40 visiting performers/producers in two 28-night rotations; 288 annual arena hours and 900 studio hours. The annual, peak-guest and crew-hour checks do not qualify a day-by-day timetable. Eight spare arrival places and 248 spare arena hours in the reference leave little scheduling slack.",
    "capital": "An additional 30 million U studio/production allowance, with inherited 20-year renewal and 70% import share; no construction quote or funding commitment.",
    "shock": "All remote earned revenue halves with the expanded programme, opening audience and payroll unchanged; fixed operations and acquisition continue and variable commercial services fall with delivered receipts. Patron transfers are not assumed to rescue contractual obligations.",
    "income": "Foreign equity is represented as a direct-investment claim for the proportional retained-earnings memo. Dispersed portfolio equity needs its own statistical treatment. Artists modeled as employees; independent offworld production companies instead sell imported services."
